          "description": "Updated July 2026: Complete list of 24+ operational, under-construction & proposed metro, RRTS & bullet train projects in India with route maps & status.",
          "articleText": "Last updated: 25 August 2026 · Reviewed by the Metro Rail News editorial desk India operates the world’s third-largest metro rail network. According to Ministry of Housing and Urban Affairs (MoHUA) figures placed before Parliament, 1,076.585 km of metro lines were in commercial operation across 26 cities as of July 2026, with nearly 1,000 km more under construction. This page is the complete, continuously updated index of every metro, RRTS (Namo Bharat), and high-speed rail project in India, organised by status, with network data, implementing agencies, and links to each project’s dedicated information page for route maps, tenders, contracts, and the latest news. India’s metro journey began with the Kolkata Metro in 1984, followed by the Delhi Metro in 2002, which set the template for modern urban rail delivery in the country. The network crossed the 1,000-km milestone in May 2025, and with the full 82.15 km Delhi–Meerut Namo Bharat corridor operational and the 508 km Mumbai–Ahmedabad bullet train under construction, India’s rapid transit build-out is now the largest anywhere outside China. On this page: Network at a Glance · State-wise Network · Operational Systems · Under Construction · Approved · Proposed · RRTS / Namo Bharat · High-Speed Rail · Other Rapid Rail · FAQs Figures are as of July 2026 and are updated as projects progress. Click any project for detailed route maps, phases, contractors, tenders, and news updates. The table below is the official city-wise breakdown of operational metro length, as placed before Parliament by the Ministry of Housing and Urban Affairs in July 2026. Figures are for revenue service only and exclude sections under trial or awaiting CMRS clearance. Delhi & NCR is reported as a single network because Delhi Metro, Noida Metro and Rapid Metro Gurugram operate as one contiguous system across the region. Source: Ministry of Housing and Urban Affairs, reply to Lok Sabha Unstarred Question (annexure), July 2026 — sansad.in. Twenty-one Indian cities have their own metro system in commercial passenger service; counting NCR and other satellite cities served by a neighbouring network, official figures cover 26 cities. Several of the newer systems (Agra, Indore, Bhopal, and Patna) are running priority sections while the balance of their networks remains under construction. Beyond the operational systems, entirely new metro networks are under construction in the following cities: In parallel, every major operational system is expanding. Key extension programmes under construction include Delhi Metro Phase IV (61.679 km across three priority corridors plus two additional approved corridors — civil work on the Inderlok–Indraprastha corridor began in July 2026), Chennai Metro Phase 2 (116.1 km, first section opening in 2026), Bengaluru’s Phase 2/2A/2B and Phase 3 corridors, Mumbai Metro Lines 2B, 4, 5, 6, 9 and 12, Kolkata’s Orange and Yellow line extensions, Pune Metro’s Phase 2 reaches and Line 3 to Hinjawadi (23.2 km, opening 2026), Kochi Metro Phase 2 to Infopark, Ahmedabad Metro’s newly approved Phase 2A airport corridor (6 km, sanctioned June 2026), and the balance corridors in Agra, Indore, Bhopal, Patna and Kanpur. The following projects have received state and/or central government approval and are in the pre-construction stage: land acquisition, detailed design, and tendering: DPRs have been prepared or are being prepared for metro, Metrolite, and Metro Neo systems in these cities. Timelines depend on state and MoHUA/Union Cabinet approvals: The Regional Rapid Transit System (RRTS), branded Namo Bharat, is a 160 km/h regional rail network being developed by NCRTC to connect Delhi with NCR cities. The first corridor is fully operational; two more Phase 1 corridors are approved. NHSRCL is building India’s first high-speed rail line between Mumbai and Ahmedabad using Japanese Shinkansen technology. Seven further corridors have been studied under the National Rail Plan: The stories below refresh on their own as we publish: line openings, contract awards, tenders, rolling-stock and policy news from across India’s metros. See all metro rail coverage → As of July 2026, metro trains run in commercial service in 21 Indian cities, from Delhi and Mumbai to newer systems in Agra, Indore, Bhopal, Patna and Meerut. Counting NCR satellite systems separately, MoHUA puts the figure at 26 cities with 1,076.585 km of operational metro lines. India has 1,076.585 km of operational metro lines as of July 2026, according to MoHUA figures placed before Parliament. This makes India’s metro network the third-largest in the world, and around 85 km of new lines were added in 2025 alone. Nearly 1,000 km more is under construction. Delhi Metro is India’s largest metro network at 374.5 km with 271 stations across 10 colour-coded lines, operated by DMRC. Mumbai Metro is second at about 101 km, followed by Bengaluru’s Namma Metro at 96.1 km and Kolkata Metro at 72.98 km. Kolkata Metro was India’s first metr",
