          "title": "Merlin Group plans ₹10,000 crore investment over five years; targets ₹700 crore sales in FY27",
          "description": "Merlin Group announces a significant ₹10,000 crore investment over five years to expand its real estate portfolio in West Bengal and Maharashtra, targeting ₹700 crore in sales...",
          "articleText": "Industry 5 min read Merlin Group plans ₹10,000 crore investment over five years; targets ₹700 crore sales in FY27 Merlin Group plans to deploy about ₹10,000 crore over the next five years across residential, commercial, retail and mixed-use projects, said Sushil Mohta, chairman, Merlin Group and president, CREDAI West Bengal. The developer, which recorded sales of around ₹500 crore in FY26, is targeting about ₹700 crore in FY27 and has nearly 20 million sq ft of development potential lined up for launch over the next two-three years. Ankit Sharma ETRealty Published On Oct 2, 2026 at 11:34 PM IST NEW DELHI: Merlin Group plans to invest about ₹10,000 crore over the next five years as it expands its residential, commercial, retail and mixed-use portfolio across West Bengal and Maharashtra, Sushil Mohta, chairman, Merlin Group and president, CREDAI West Bengal, told ETRealty in an exclusive interaction.The company currently deploys about ₹1,000-1,200 crore annually, with investment expected to increase by around 15-20% year-on-year. It recorded sales of approximately ₹500 crore and profit of around ₹100 crore in FY26, and is targeting sales of about ₹700 crore and profit of at least ₹120 crore in FY27. Advt Mohta was speaking on the sidelines of CREDAI NATCON 2026 being held in Kolkata.The developer has around 20 million sq ft of development potential planned for launch over the next two-three years. Its major projects include the World Trade Center development in Kolkata, new commercial and residential projects in Pune and a growing rental portfolio. Edited excerpts:How are you looking at the Kolkata and West Bengal real estate market in 2026?Real estate follows economic growth. If industrial investment and employment increase, housing and commercial real estate will follow. We are seeing interest from large industrial houses as well as MSMEs, and that should eventually translate into greater real estate demand.Apart from Kolkata, Siliguri is growing rapidly. North Bengal is also seeing significant hospitality and tourism development. Durgapur and Asansol are other markets that can grow.Kolkata itself is expanding beyond its traditional boundaries, with areas such as Howrah, New Town, Barrackpore and the southern corridor towards Diamond Harbour developing rapidly.Which housing segments are seeing the strongest demand?Premium and sub-premium housing will continue to grow as wealth creation increases, but Kolkata remains a predominantly mid-income market. The ₹50 lakh to ₹1 crore segment accounts for around 80-90% of supply. Advt There is also considerable demand below ₹50 lakh, but the economics of developing housing in that price category have become increasingly difficult because construction costs have risen substantially.How much have construction costs increased?Costs have increased by around 20% during the year, largely due to higher prices of materials such as aluminium, tiles and other construction inputs.In some projects, margins have been completely eroded because under RERA we cannot increase prices for units that have already been sold.Price increases can only be applied to new sales or new projects. If costs increase after a sale has already been committed, the developer has to absorb that increase.What has been the outcome of the recent structural audits of projects in Kolkata?RERA has already brought substantial accountability and transparency into the sector through disclosure requirements, carpet-area definitions, delivery commitments and warranties.On the recent structural audit, most of the work was completed within around 30 days with universities and agencies such as RITES involved.Around 26 projects were found with significant violations, according to our understanding, and these were largely small suburban projects. None of the projects belonging to our CREDAI members had an issue. Around 600-670 projects have now been cleared and construction has restarted, with work having resumed for almost a month.What are Merlin Group's expansion plans over the next five years?We have projects involving around ₹10,000 crore of investment over the next five years. One of our largest projects is the World Trade Center in Kolkata, which is a mixed-use commercial development of around four million sq ft, comprising IT and GCC office space, a five-star deluxe hotel and food and beverage retail.Phase 1 is around 600,000 sq ft and is almost completely sold or leased. We also have a mall of approximately 400,000 sq ft in Joka, apart from our residential portfolio.What are your residential volumes?We sell around 1,000-1,200 residential units annually, with the primary focus remaining on mid-income housing. We have already launched two projects during 2026.At the World Trade Center development, phase 2 is around 1.5 million sq ft and phase 3 around one million sq ft. We intend to launch at least one additional phase during 2026.What are your completion plans?During 2026, we expect to complete several projects",
