          "title": "Old legislations impeding development must go: West Bengal finance minister",
          "description": "West Bengal's Finance Minister Swapan Dasgupta advocates for the removal of outdated land legislations like the Urban Land Ceiling Act to facilitate real estate and...",
          "articleText": "Industry 2 min read Old legislations impeding development must go: West Bengal finance minister West Bengal finance minister Swapan Dasgupta said legacy land laws, including the Urban Land (Ceiling and Regulation) Act, need to be reviewed or removed where they restrict development, as the state seeks to attract organised developers and industrial investment. He also called for greater regulation, accountability and transparency in the real estate sector. Ankit Sharma ETRealty Updated On Oct 2, 2026 at 11:27 PM IST Swapan Dasgupta, finance minister, West Bengal at CREDAI NATCON 2026NEW DELHI: West Bengal finance minister Swapan Dasgupta said older land legislations that act as impediments to development need to be removed as the state seeks to facilitate larger real estate, industrial and infrastructure projects.Speaking at CREDAI NATCON 2026 in Kolkata, Dasgupta specifically referred to the Urban Land (Ceiling and Regulation) Act, 1976, saying the legislation has become a deterrent to development and “has to go”. His remarks reiterate the state government's stated intention to scrap the law to unlock land and attract investment. Advt “We have certain laws which date back to the 70s, which the rest of India has scrapped. It is an impediment and a deterrent to development. It has to go,” he said.Dasgupta also referred to provisions of the West Bengal Land Reforms Act, 1955, as part of the wider land-law framework that the state needs to address as it attempts to facilitate industrial and urban development.“As we build more factories and logistical bases, these Acts will be a hindrance to growth,” Dasgupta said.The state government has separately been looking at measures to improve the availability of land for industry, including the creation of an investment-ready land bank using parcels belonging to closed public-sector units, sick industries and other available properties.“We need to upgrade at every level. We need to catch up with other states,” Dasgupta said, positioning modernisation and investment as priorities for West Bengal.He also called for a larger role for organised real estate developers, arguing that stronger regulation and accountability would be necessary as the sector expands.Dasgupta said real estate in the past had at times been dominated by unorganised operators who had shortchanged homebuyers, and that the sector now required “better regulation, accountability and transparency”. He said implementation of RERA and greater participation by organised developers would be important to that process. Advt The Urban Land Ceiling Act has been a long-standing concern for developers operating in West Bengal, who have argued that landholding restrictions make aggregation of larger parcels difficult and constrain large-scale development. The state had previously examined the continued applicability of the legislation as well as ceiling provisions under the West Bengal Land Reforms Act. By Ankit Sharma , ETRealty Published On Oct 2, 2026 at 11:24 PM IST See more on: Swapan Dasgupta, West Bengal finance minister, Urban Land Ceiling Act, CREDAI NATCON 2026, West Bengal, Kolkata Join the community of 2M+ industry professionals. Subscribe to Newsletter to get latest insights & analysis in your inbox. All about ETRealty industry right on your smartphone! Download the ETRealty App and get the Realtime updates and Save your favourite articles. HomeNewsIndustryOld legislations impeding development must go: West Bengal finance minister Industry 2 min read Old legislations impeding development must go: West Bengal finance minister West Bengal finance minister Swapan Dasgupta said legacy land laws, including the Urban Land (Ceiling and Regulation) Act, need to be reviewed or removed where they restrict development, as the state seeks to attract organised developers and industrial investment. He also called for greater regulation, accountability and transparency in the real estate sector. Industry 2 min rea