          "title": "Bengaluru is now a water-surplus city: V Ram Prasath Manohar, Chairman, BWSSB",
          "description": "Bengaluru in 2024 faced severe water shortages, putting a maximum strain on the residents of India’s IT capital. Since then Bangalore Water Supply and Sewer..",
          "articleText": "Bengaluru is now a water-surplus city: V Ram Prasath Manohar, Chairman, BWSSB Bengaluru in 2024 faced severe water shortages, putting a maximum strain on the residents of India’s IT capital. Since then Bangalore Water Supply and Sewerage Board (BWSSB) has undertaken a number of measures to make the city water secure and resilient for the present as well as the future. V Ram Prasath Manohar, Chairman, BWSSB, in a conversation with ET Infra about Bengaluru's journey from facing a severe water crisis a few years ago to achieving water surplus, driven by innovative management strategies, technological adoption, and community engagement. Manohar outlines what are the measures that the board is undertaking to address non-revenue water and adoption of digital technologies for ensuring efficient water distribution. He talks about how the board has undertaken a staggered pricing policy to ensure affordability, especially for lower-income segments, while covering costs for higher consumption. In the future, to ensure a robust and financially sustainable water sector development in Bengaluru, Manohar highlights that attracting private equity and undertaking development in the water sector in the PPP mode are being focused on. Dev Kachari ETInfra Updated On May 5, 2026 at 07:02 PM IST Watched by 497 Professionals Advertisement See more on: V Ram Prasath Manohar, bengaluru water crisis, bwssb, water management, non-revenue water, ppp water sector, ETInfra, Join the community of 2M+ industry professionals. Subscribe to Newsletter to get latest insights & analysis in your inbox. All about ETInfra industry right on your smartphone! Download the ETInfra App and get the Realtime updates and Save your favourite articles. HomeVideosBengaluru is now a water-surplus city: V Ram Prasath Manohar, Chairman, BWSSB Bengaluru is now a water-surplus city: V Ram Prasath Manohar, Chairman, BWSSB Bengaluru in 2024 faced severe water shortages, putting a maximum strain on 