          "title": "Haryana Property Transfer to Family: What the 2026 Stamp Duty Clarification Means",
          "description": "Buyers need to know this - Haryana's 100% stamp-duty exemption for eligible family property transfers, including grandchildren through daughters.",
          "articleText": "Last Updated: August 2026Property is often transferred within families for several reasons that may have little to do with a conventional sale. Parents transfer a home to their children, grandparents want to pass property to grandchildren, or families want to put long-term ownership arrangements in place during the owner’s lifetime.In Haryana, such transfers can benefit from a significant stamp-duty exemption. Table of Contents Toggle Featured Projects More projects → BPTP GAIA Residences Gurugram, Haryana From ₹38,500,000 Godrej Alira Gurugram, Haryana From ₹60,000,000 Signature Global Cloverdale SPR Gurugram, Haryana From ₹38,799,997 A recent government clarification has removed an ambiguity over one category of family transfers: transfers to grandchildren through daughters.The clarification is particularly relevant for families considering a gift or other eligible lifetime property transfer in Haryana.What Has Haryana Clarified?The Haryana government has confirmed that its 100% stamp-duty exemption on eligible lifetime transfers of immovable property within specified blood relations also covers grandchildren through daughters.This is not a new exemption introduced in 2026.The underlying exemption dates back to a Haryana government notification issued on June 16, 2014, under Section 9 of the Indian Stamp Act, 1899. It provided for full stamp-duty remission on eligible property transfer deeds executed during the owner’s lifetime in favour of specified blood relations, including parents, children, grandchildren, siblings and spouses.The problem arose because the Hindi version of the notification used wording that specifically referred to a son’s children. The broader English wording referred to “grandchildren”.That difference created uncertainty about whether grandchildren through daughters were covered.What Changed in 2026?To remove the ambiguity, Haryana’s Revenue and Disaster Management Department issued a corrigendum dated July 24, 2026.It was subsequently published in the Haryana Government Gazette on August 13, 2026.The amended wording explicitly includes grandchildren through daughters, placing them on the same footing as grandchildren through sons for the purpose of the stamp-duty exemption.The government has described the clarification as providing retrospective legal clarity across revenue and registration offices.In practical terms, eligible families should now have greater clarity when approaching registration authorities for such transfers.Who Does the Exemption Apply To?The government clarification relates to eligible lifetime transfers of immovable property within specified blood relations.The original 2014 notification covered relationships including:parents; children; grandchildren; siblings; and spouses.The 2026 clarification specifically addresses the position of a daughter’s children within the category of grandchildren.This is important because the exemption is relationship-specific. It should not be interpreted as a blanket exemption on every property transfer between every family member.The exact relationship, nature of the transfer and documentation should therefore be confirmed before proceeding.Isn’t This the Same as Selling a Property?No.A family transfer can take several legal forms, including a gift, settlement or other instrument, depending on the circumstances.The applicable documentation and stamp-duty treatment can differ according to the nature of the transaction.Haryana’s Revenue and Disaster Management Department provides official deed templates, including gift, family settlement, release, partition and sale deeds, through its registration resources.This is why families should establish the appropriate legal instrument before simply assuming that a transfer qualifies for the exemption.What Should Families Do Before Transferring Property?A stamp-duty exemption does not remove the need for proper documentation.Before executing a family transfer, check:1. Who legally owns the property?The transferor must have the legal right to transfer the property.2. What is the exact relationship between transferor and recipient?The exemption applies to specified relationships. The relationship should be clearly established through the relevant documents.3. What type of transfer is being made?A gift deed, family settlement, release or other instrument can have different legal implications.4. Are the property documents in order?The title documents, property description and relevant revenue or municipal records should be checked before the transfer.5. Has the applicable stamp-duty treatment been confirmed?The exemption should be verified with the relevant registration authority or a property lawyer before execution.Haryana’s official registration guidance states that documents presented for registration require relevant enclosures, including title deed and Jamabandi where applicable, along with the required property description and other documents.Why This Matters to Property Owners in Gurg",
          "newsLink": "https://savemax.in/blogs/haryana-property-transfer-stamp-duty-waiver/",
          "sourceUrl": "https://savemax.in/blogs/feed",
          "publishedAt": "2026-08-24T07:26:37.000Z",
          "createdAt": "2026-08-24T07:26:37.000Z",
