        {
          "title": "Property Registration Does Not Prove Clear Ownership: What Gurgaon Buyers Must Check",
          "description": "Property registration is not the whole ownership story. Learn what you should check in the title chain, encumbrances and property records.",
          "articleText": "Congratulations!The property has been registered, and the sale deed is in your name. And you heave a sigh of relief that the transaction is complete. Table of Contents Toggle Featured Projects More projects → BPTP GAIA Residences Gurugram, Haryana From ₹38,500,000 Godrej Alira Gurugram, Haryana From ₹60,000,000 Signature Global Cloverdale SPR Gurugram, Haryana From ₹38,799,997 Does that automatically mean there can be no ownership problem later?Unfortunately, not necessarily.Registration is an essential part of buying property, but it should not be confused with complete title verification.A recent report highlights an important point for homebuyers: a registered sale deed records the transaction, but registry by itself does not cure defects in the seller’s underlying title. Buyers still need to examine the ownership chain, encumbrances, approvals and other relevant records before completing a purchase.For buyers in Gurgaon, this is one of the most important legal checks to understand before paying a substantial amount towards a property.What Does Property Registration Actually Mean?Registration formally records the transaction with the appropriate authority.Haryana’s Revenue and Disaster Management Department requires documents relating to immovable property to be presented before the relevant Sub-Registrar and sets out the documentation and registration process.But the onus is on the buyer to distinguish between:A: Registration of the transactionandB: Verification of the seller’s title.They are related, but they are not the same exercise.Why the Seller’s Title MattersSuppose you are buying a resale apartment.The seller gives you the current sale deed showing that they purchased the property several years ago.That is important.But how did the seller acquire it? And if they were not the first owners of the property, how did the previous owner acquire it?A proper title review examines the chain of ownership, rather than stopping with the latest document.We recommend that buyers examine previous title documents and ownership records, particularly where a property has changed hands multiple times.Haryana’s own registration guidance distinguishes a title deed from an agreement to sell and makes clear that a Power of Attorney is not itself a title deed.The Documents Buyers Should Look BeyondThe exact documents required depend on the property and the way it was acquired, but a buyer may need to examine:Previous Sale or Conveyance DeedsThese establish how ownership moved from one person to another.Look for gaps or inconsistencies in the chain.Mother DeedWhere applicable, the mother deed and earlier documents can help establish the property’s ownership history.Encumbrance RecordsAn Encumbrance Certificate can help identify registered mortgages, liens or other registered claims relating to the property for the relevant period.However, an EC should not be treated as an absolute guarantee that no possible dispute exists. Some claims or liabilities may not appear in registration records.Mutation and Revenue or Municipal RecordsThese records can provide another layer of verification.But mutation itself should not be treated as conclusive proof of ownership.The important question is whether the underlying title and transfer documents establish the seller’s legal right to sell.Property Tax RecordsCheck whether applicable property taxes and other dues have been paid.Outstanding dues can create complications after purchase.Don’t Forget the Building DocumentsTitle is only one part of due diligence.For a constructed property, buyers should also examine applicable:sanctioned building plans; approvals; completion or occupancy documentation, where applicable; RERA registration for projects that fall within its scope; society or RWA records, where relevant; and authority or allotment documents, depending on the property.This becomes particularly important when buying an apartment, builder floor or property that has undergone construction or alteration.What About Properties That Have Been Inherited?Inherited properties require additional care.The ownership trail may involve:a Will; succession documents; probate or court orders, where applicable; gift or settlement deeds; partition documents; or family settlement arrangements.The current seller’s name appearing in one document may therefore not tell the whole story.If inheritance or family arrangements form part of the property’s history, an independent legal review becomes even more important.What Should a Gurgaon Buyer Do Before Paying?A sensible due-diligence process should happen before the transaction becomes irreversible.Start by establishing who owns the property.Then work backwards through the title chain.Check for registered encumbrances and outstanding dues.Verify the property’s approvals and physical description.For a developer project, check the project’s RERA information and relevant approvals.And for a high-value or complicated transaction, consider obtaining an indep",
